fornthulani-gen
===============

Project generator for the
[fornthulani](https://crates.io/crates/fornthulani) micro HTTP framework.

Installs a binary called `fornthulani`. Run `fornthulani generate …`
from any directory to scaffold a project or add a route, controller,
or plugin to an existing one.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALL
----------------------------------------------------------------------

From crates.io (once published):

  cargo install fornthulani-gen

From source:

  git clone https://github.com/thomas666-beast/fornthulani-gen
  cd fornthulani-gen
  cargo install --path . --force

After install:

  fornthulani --version
  fornthulani --help


----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMMANDS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  fornthulani generate app <name>              create a new project
  fornthulani generate route <name>            add a route + controller
  fornthulani generate controller <name>       add only a controller
  fornthulani generate plugin <name>           add a Middleware plugin

All `route`, `controller`, and `plugin` commands look for the nearest
`Cargo.toml` with a `src/main.rs` file — so you run them from inside
the project, from any subdirectory.

`app` is the only command that creates a new directory; run it from
wherever you want the project to live.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERATE APP
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd ~/projects
  fornthulani generate app my-app --fornthulani-path ../fornthulani

Produces:

  my-app/
    Cargo.toml
    fornthulani.toml
    README.md
    .gitignore
    src/
      main.rs
      app.rs
      config.rs
      routes/
        mod.rs
        hello.rs
      controllers/
        mod.rs
        hello.rs
      plugins/
        mod.rs

Then:

  cd my-app
  cargo run

and in another terminal:

  curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/hello
  # hello, fornthulani

The generated project has one working route (`GET /hello`) and one
working controller (`controllers/hello.rs`). It reads its host, port,
and default content type from `fornthulani.toml`.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
DEPENDENCY FLAGS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

`fornthulani generate app` accepts one of two flags to control how
the generated project depends on `fornthulani`:

  --fornthulani-path <path>       reference a local checkout
                                  (default: ../fornthulani)

  --fornthulani-version <ver>     reference a crates.io version
                                  (use this after fornthulani is
                                  published)

Examples:

  # local development — clones of fornthulani and my-app side by side
  fornthulani generate app my-app --fornthulani-path ../fornthulani

  # after fornthulani is published
  fornthulani generate app my-app --fornthulani-version 0.1.0

The two flags are mutually exclusive. If neither is given, `--fornthulani-path ../fornthulani`
is used.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERATE ROUTE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd my-app
  fornthulani generate route users

Produces:

  src/routes/users.rs          the route wiring
  src/controllers/users.rs     the handler

And appends:

  src/routes/mod.rs       ->  pub mod users;
                              users::register(router);
  src/controllers/mod.rs  ->  pub mod users;

The new route is `GET /users` returning the text "users". Open
`src/controllers/users.rs` and edit the function to do whatever
you need.

If `src/routes/users.rs` already exists, the command refuses to
overwrite it. Use a different name, or delete the file first.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERATE CONTROLLER
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd my-app
  fornthulani generate controller health

Produces:

  src/controllers/health.rs

And appends:

  src/controllers/mod.rs  ->  pub mod health;

Useful when you want the logic separate from the route registration
(e.g. one controller used by multiple routes, or a controller you
will wire by hand).


----------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERATE PLUGIN
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  cd my-app
  fornthulani generate plugin auth

Produces:

  src/plugins/auth.rs     a struct Auth implementing fornthulani::Middleware

And appends:

  src/plugins/mod.rs  ->  pub mod auth;

The generated file looks like:

  use fornthulani::{Middleware, Request, Response, ThulaniError};

  pub struct Auth;

  impl Middleware for Auth {
      fn before(&self, _req: &mut Request) -> Result<Option<Response>, ThulaniError> {
          // inspect or reject the request here
          Ok(None)
      }

      fn after(&self, _req: &Request, _resp: &mut Response) {
          // inspect or mutate the response here
      }
  }

To use it, edit `src/main.rs` and add:

  use crate::plugins::auth::Auth;

  let server = Server::new(router)
      .with_middleware(Auth);

Or attach it to specific routes in `src/routes/<name>.rs`:

  router.get("/admin", admin_handler).with(Auth);


----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROJECT LAYOUT REFERENCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Every generated project has the same shape:

  src/
    main.rs           entry point; builds Server, attaches global middleware
    app.rs            builds Router from routes::register
    config.rs         reads fornthulani.toml
    routes/           one file per route group
    controllers/      one file per endpoint
    plugins/          developer-written Middleware

When you add features with `generate route` / `generate plugin`, the
new files go into the right subfolder and both `mod.rs` files are
updated automatically. You never edit `mod.rs` by hand.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
FORNTHULANI.TOML
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The generated config file has three optional sections:

  [server]
  host = "127.0.0.1"
  port = 8080

  [response]
  default_content_type = "application/json"

  # Add your own sections — the generated config.rs parses them into
  # a Config struct, so you control what goes here.
  [auth]
  api_key = "secret-demo-key"

Every field is optional. Missing values fall back to the defaults
shown above.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
DESIGN NOTES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  - The generator never modifies fornthulani/src/. It only writes files
    in the target project.
  - Existing files are never overwritten. If a route, controller, or
    plugin with the same name exists, the command fails with an error.
  - `mod.rs` files are updated by appending, never by rewriting.
    This means manual edits inside mod.rs survive.
  - Controllers are `fn` pointers. That is why generate route writes a
    standalone function and wires it in a separate file — the two
    concerns (URL space vs. handler logic) stay separated.
  - All paths are relative to the nearest Cargo.toml. Running generate
    from a subdirectory works.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The binary is a thin wrapper around two things:

  - clap for argument parsing
  - embedded string templates for the generated files

There is no template engine, no file watcher, no codegen pipeline.
Each generator produces plain Rust source you can edit freely.
If you outgrow the scaffolding, delete the generated file and write
your own — nothing in fornthulani depends on the generator.


